        "tapescript": "PART 2 – (Questions 6–10) A radio report about a new type of cargo ship\nPresenter: Good morning, and welcome to the business report. Today we look at a new kind of cargo ship that has just finished its first season at sea. The Vindur, built by the Norwegian company Nordhavn Maritime, is a bulk carrier fitted with four tall rotating cylinders, an idea first tried in the 1920s, which use the wind to help push the ship forward.\nThe project’s chief engineer, Lars Moen, says that beating other ships on speed was never the point.\nLars Moen: “Everyone assumes a ship with sails must be slower, and in fact it is, slightly. But on long ocean voyages shipping companies spend a fortune on fuel, and that is the cost we wanted to cut. Faster crossings or bigger holds were never our aim, and the crew is the same size as on a conventional vessel.”\nPresenter: The Vindur doesn’t simply follow the shortest route. An onboard system compares weather forecasts, and where a detour of a few hundred kilometres would put the ship in stronger, steadier winds, it takes that route. The journey is longer, but the fuel saved more than makes up for the extra distance. Storms are a different matter: the cylinders can be stopped within seconds, so the captain doesn’t need to alter course just to protect them.\nOf course, the wind isn’t always blowing, so the Vindur also carries a conventional engine. It doesn’t merely take over in calm weather. It keeps the ship moving at a steady pace the whole time and generates the electricity for lighting, navigation and refrigerated cargo, so nothing ever has to be switched off.\nThe project got an unexpected boost when a major Japanese company, which has no background in shipping, asked to take part. The cylinders have to be extremely light, yet strong enough to survive constant spinning, and the firm specialises in precisely that kind of composite material, which it already makes for trains and aircraft. It is not in the business of building engines or electronic equipment.\nSo will other shipping lines follow? Mr Moen is careful not to promise anything.\nLars Moen: “Ask me again once we’ve seen the accounts after our first full year at sea. If the ships do what the figures say they should, the orders will come.”",
